<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albrecht von Haller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1708">1708</a>–<a href="/years/1777">1777</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albrecht von Haller was a Swiss physiologist, anatomist, botanist and poet from Bern, often called the father of modern physiology for his experiments on the irritability of muscles and the sensibility of nerves. He taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> from 1736 to 1753. His poem Die Alpen of 1729 helped create the image of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and their people as simple and virtuous.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-haller.jpg" alt="Albrecht von Haller" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Rudolf Huber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_von_Haller_1736.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Haller was born on 16 October 1708 into an old Swiss family at Bern. Long ill-health kept him from boyish sports and gave his precocious mind more room. It is said that at four he read and expounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> to his father's servants, and before he was ten he had sketched a Biblical Aramaic grammar. Before he was fifteen he had written metrical translations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, lyrics, dramas and an epic of four thousand lines on the origin of the Swiss confederations, which he burned in 1729. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-boerhaave">Herman Boerhaave</a>, and graduated in May 1727.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1728 he went to Basel, where he studied higher mathematics under John Bernoulli and where his interest in botany was awakened. A tour through Savoy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and Swiss cantons began the plant collection that became the basis of his work on the flora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In 1729 he returned to Bern to practise as a physician. His botanical and anatomical research gave him a European reputation, and in 1736 George II called him to the chair of medicine, anatomy, botany and surgery at the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a>. He became a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1743 and was ennobled in 1749.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> he organised a botanical garden and an anatomical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and edited the monthly Göttingische gelehrte Anzeigen, to which he is said to have contributed twelve thousand articles. Never at home there, and elected to the great council of Bern in 1745, he resigned his chair in 1753 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In his last years he held the minor post of Rathausmann and prepared his Bibliotheca medica. He withdrew from public business in about 1773 and relied on opium, whose excessive use is believed to have hastened his death on 12 December 1777.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In his thesis published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> in 1752 he distinguished sensibility from irritability: nerves were sensible because a person perceives contact, while muscle fibre was irritable because it measurably shortens when excited, whether or not the person perceives it. In 1757 he carried out experiments to distinguish nerve impulses from muscular contractions. In botany he was among the first to see the importance of herbaria for studying variation, and he resisted the binomial nomenclature <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a> introduced in 1753.</p><p>His poem Die Alpen, of 490 hexameters, was finished in March 1729 and appeared in his Gedichte in 1732. It is one of the earliest signs of an appreciation of the mountains and contrasts the simple life of people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> with the corrupt life of the plains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albrecht von Haller” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…its university founded in 1460 and printers such as Johann Froben, became a centre of humanism where Erasmus lived and Hans Holbein the Younger worked, and in the eighteenth century the Bernoulli family, Leonhard Euler and <b>Albrecht von Haller</b> gave the Confederacy a leading place in European science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bern, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Bern, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.95,7.45&amp;year=1752" aria-label="Bern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4725 515 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albrecht von Haller"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5175 785h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5175 785h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.95,7.45&amp;year=1752">What was here in 1752? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
